      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Against the <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill">NordicTrack Commercial 1750</a>,
          the honest summary is this: you're giving up a slightly stronger
          motor and a frame built to a marginally higher duty standard, and
          in exchange you're saving roughly $200 while keeping the same deck
          size, incline range, and touchscreen ecosystem. For moderate home
          use, that's a genuinely good trade. Against the
          <a href="/proform-power-1295i-treadmill">ProForm Smart Power 1295i</a>,
          the Pro 2000 is the better choice for taller runners and anyone
          training seriously, thanks to its larger deck and stronger motor —
          but the 1295i is worth a look if you want to save another few
          hundred dollars and don't need the full-size deck.
        </p>
        <p>
          One more comparison worth making mentally: ProForm's own entry
          and mid-tier machines, like the Power 995i, which typically run
          $999-$1,099. The Pro 2000's larger deck, stronger motor, and
          bigger touchscreen justify the price gap for anyone who runs
          regularly, but if your household mostly walks, the 995i covers
          the basics at a meaningfully lower price. We break down that
          entire relationship in our
          <a href="/proform-treadmill">ProForm brand guide</a>.
        </p>

