        <h2 id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">Who Should Skip This Treadmill</h2>
        <p>
          The Pro 2000 is a strong value, but it isn't the right machine
          for every shopper who lands on this page. If you run daily and
          expect to put serious mileage on the machine for years, the extra
          durability margin of the NordicTrack Commercial 1750 is worth the
          roughly $200 premium — that's exactly the kind of use case where
          the lighter-duty motor and frame are most likely to show their
          limits sooner. If you live in an apartment and need the treadmill
          to genuinely disappear between uses, its large folded footprint
          will frustrate you more than it helps; a dedicated compact
          folding treadmill from our
          <a href="/best-folding-treadmill-reviews">folding treadmill guide</a>
          will serve you better. And if you know for certain you'll never
          pay for an iFit subscription, you're paying for touchscreen
          hardware you'll rarely use at full value — a simpler
          console-based machine, or ProForm's own Power 995i, may be the
          smarter buy from our
          <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews">budget treadmill guide</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">Final Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          The ProForm Pro 2000 is one of the more honest value propositions
          we've tested in the mid-range treadmill category. It doesn't
          pretend to be the Commercial 1750, but it also doesn't need to —
          it matches the deck size, the incline range, and the iFit
          software that most home users actually interact with day to day,
          while asking for meaningfully less money. The trade-offs are real
          and worth taking seriously: a slightly smaller motor, a
          lighter-duty frame, and small polish differences like touchscreen
          wake time. None of them are dealbreakers for the moderate home
          user this machine is built for.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you've read this far and you're weighing the Pro 2000 against
          the Commercial 1750 specifically, our advice is simple: buy the
          Pro 2000 if your training is consistent but not extreme, and step
          up to the Commercial 1750 if you're running most days of the week
          and want the extra durability margin that comes with NordicTrack's
          slightly heavier-duty build.
        </p>
