        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the ProForm Pro 2000 basically the same treadmill as the NordicTrack Commercial 1750?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              They're closely related but not identical. Both share a
              parent company, iFit Health & Fitness, and both run on the
              same 22 x 60-inch deck, -3% to 12% incline range, and iFit
              software. The Pro 2000's 3.6 CHP motor is a touch smaller
              than the Commercial 1750's 3.75 CHP, and the frame and
              components underneath the shell are generally lighter-duty
              on the ProForm side, which is exactly how ProForm is able to
              sell it for roughly $200 less.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the ProForm Pro 2000 come with iFit included?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Yes, it ships with a one-year iFit membership, the same
              arrangement NordicTrack uses on its Commercial line. Most of
              the touchscreen's standout features — auto-adjusting incline
              during trainer-led runs, the full workout library, live and
              on-demand classes — require an active membership, so budget
              for the subscription cost once the included year runs out.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How does the Pro 2000 compare to the ProForm Smart Power 1295i?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              The Pro 2000 is the better machine for taller runners and
              anyone training seriously, thanks to its larger 22 x 60-inch
              deck versus the 1295i's 20 x 60-inch deck and a slightly
              stronger motor. The 1295i is around $300 cheaper and still
              gets a 12-inch touchscreen and 12% incline, making it the
              better pick if deck size isn't a dealbreaker for your height
              and stride.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the ProForm Pro 2000's deck big enough for running, not just walking?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Yes. The 22 x 60-inch deck matches the NordicTrack Commercial
              1750's footprint and comfortably fit our taller testers
              during full-stride running. This is one of the areas where
              the Pro 2000 doesn't cut corners relative to its more
              expensive NordicTrack sibling.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the ProForm Pro 2000 as durable as the NordicTrack Commercial 1750?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Not quite, and ProForm doesn't really claim otherwise. The
              two machines share a parent company and a lot of the same
              software and design language, but ProForm's hardware sits a
              step below NordicTrack's Commercial tier in frame gauge and
              motor duty rating. For moderate home use — walking, jogging,
              a few interval sessions a week — the difference is unlikely
              to matter within the warranty window. For daily heavy
              mileage, the Commercial 1750's sturdier build is the safer
              long-term bet.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does assembly take on the ProForm Pro 2000?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Around 70 minutes working alone, or closer to 40 minutes with
              a second person helping lift the upright console mast into
              place, which the manual recommends. The layout and hardware
              are similar enough to NordicTrack's assembly process that
              anyone who has built a NordicTrack machine before will find
              the steps familiar.
            </p>
